Usage: xargs [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Build and execute command lines from standard input.

Items are separated by blanks. The result command line is executed
after each group of max-args items is read.

OPTIONS:
  -n, --max-args NUM          use at most max-args arguments per command line
  -I ARG                      replace occurrences of replace-str in the
                              initial-arguments with names
  -i, --replace[=ARG]         deprecated alias for -I; replace occurrences of
                              replace-str
  -0, --null                  input items are terminated by a null character
  -d, --delimiter ARG         input items are terminated by the specified
                              character
  -o, --open-tty              reopen standard input as the console in the child
                              process
  -t, --verbose               print the command line on the standard error
                              before executing it
  -p, --interactive           prompt before running each command line
  -r, --no-run-if-empty       if no arguments were read from standard input, do
                              not run the command
  -P, --max-procs NUM         run up to max-procs processes at a time
  -s, --max-chars NUM         use at most max-chars chars per command line
  -x, --exit                  exit if the size (see -s) is exceeded
  -L NUM                      use at most max-lines nonblank input lines per
                              command line
      --max-lines[=NUM]       use at most max-lines nonblank input lines per
                              command line
  -l[=NUM]                    deprecated alias for -L; use max-lines nonblank
                              input lines
  -a, --arg-file ARG          read items from file instead of standard input
  -E ARG                      set the logical EOF string
      --eof[=ARG]             set or disable the logical EOF string
  -e[=ARG]                    deprecated alias for --eof
      --show-limits           display command-line length limits
      --process-slot-var ARG  set an environment variable to the child process
                              slot
      --help                  display this help and exit
  -V, --version               output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
